9":12479.0,"2019-20":13484.0,"2020-21":14948.0,"2021-22":18437.0,"2022-23":14029.0,"2023-24":15766.0,"2024-25":13982.0},"prov":{"description":"NET current expenditure - NET REVENUE EXPENDITURE (TOTAL OF LINES 800 TO 804)","form":"RS","row":"805","col":"1"}}]}],"context":[{"label":"Resident population","value":110727,"year":"2020 est.","kind":"int"},{"label":"GVA (balanced, total)","value":4823000.0,"year":"2023","kind":"money"},{"label":"GVA per head","value":42970,"year":"2023","kind":"gbp"},{"label":"GDP per head","value":46930,"year":"2023","kind":"gbp"},{"label":"Band D council tax (area bill)","value":2456.13,"year":"2026-27","kind":"gbp"},{"label":"Band D \u2014 council's own element","value":283.86,"year":"2026-27","kind":"gbp"}]}},"dossier":{"summary":null,"compliance":null,"documents":[],"read_documents":[],"calendar":null,"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"The councils have governance gaps on capital management that internal audit has flagged.","lead":"Admitting past non-compliance with the Prudential Code and ongoing refinement of approvals processes suggests prior capital decisions were inadequately scrutinised. This is directly relevant to assessing whether current borrowing forecasts and project\u2026","iso":"2026-07-14","paper_title":"Capital Strategy 2026-2029.docx","paper_url":"https://democracy.adur-worthing.gov.uk/documents/s17257/17257.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Critical building assets have emergency closures or significant maintenance backlogs that the council cannot fully fund.","lead":"Major public assets in poor condition signal stretched maintenance budgets and operational risk. Pier closure and car park closures directly affect service delivery and income. The shift to reactive maintenance is visible in the railing pause.","iso":"2026-07-14","paper_title":"Capital Strategy 2026-2029.docx","paper_url":"https://democracy.adur-worthing.gov.uk/documents/s17257/17257.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Worthing requested Exceptional Financial Support from central government; the capital strategy is now built on that foundation.","lead":"EFS is a public signal of financial failure. A council that has sought exceptional support is demonstrating that mainstream financing cannot cover recurrent pressures. The capital strategy flowing from it may optimistic about funding sources or sustainability.","iso":"2026-07-14","paper_title":"Capital Strategy 2026-2029.docx","paper_url":"https://democracy.adur-worthing.gov.uk/documents/s17257/17257.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-07-14","committee":"Joint Strategic Committee","paper_title":"Capital Strategy 2026-2029.docx","paper_url":"https://democracy.adur-worthing.gov.uk/documents/s17257/17257.pdf","headline":"The councils have governance gaps on capital management that internal audit has flagged.","detail":"The document states: 'The Councils affirm their compliance with all requirements of the Prudential Code (the Code), although it's acknowledged that this has not always been the case.' It also notes 'A review of this process has been undertaken in developing this Capital Strategy, and in response to the recommendations of the internal auditor.' The new governance process 'continues to bed in' and 'will no doubt continue to develop and refine the approvals process in coming months.' This signals that capital governance was weak and has only recently been tightened.","category":"governance","matters":"Admitting past non-compliance with the Prudential Code and ongoing refinement of approvals processes suggests prior capital decisions were inadequately scrutinised. This is directly relevant to assessing whether current borrowing forecasts and project delivery are reliable.","figures":"No quantitative figures, but governance issues are acknowledged as recent and ongoing.","quote":"The Councils affirm their compliance with all requirements of the Prudential Code (the Code), although it's acknowledged that this has not always been the case. A review of this process has been undertaken in developing this Capital Strategy, and in response to the recommendations of the internal auditor.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-14","committee":"Joint Strategic Committee","paper_title":"Capital Strategy 2026-2029.docx","paper_url":"https://democracy.adur-worthing.gov.uk/documents/s17257/17257.pdf","headline":"Critical building assets have emergency closures or significant maintenance backlogs that the council cannot fully fund.","detail":"Worthing Pier had emergency closure in 2024. Grafton Multi-Storey Car Park is life-expired and has been closed 'despite decades of ongoing maintenance at great cost'. The Connaught Theatre, Splashpoint Leisure Centre, and Pavilion Theatre require building readiness works. Both councils identify 'critical maintenance issues such as asbestos management' as driving their capital spending. East Beach Chalets windows are corroding. High Street multi-storey car park railing replacement has been paused after 'tragic incidents' in 2025 to review edge protection. This is not routine maintenance \u2014 it is deferred spending on essential safety.","category":"capital","matters":"Major public assets in poor condition signal stretched maintenance budgets and operational risk. Pier closure and car park closures directly affect service delivery and income. The shift to reactive maintenance is visible in the railing pause.","figures":"General fund capital: Worthing \u00a31.5m, Adur \u00a31.0m per year. HRA capital (Adur): \u00a310.08m per year, with \u00a33.5m from reserves and remainder from mix of sources.","quote":"This current baseline reflects the need to carefully invest in the Council's assets, specifically addressing critical maintenance issues such as asbestos management and ensuring the general safety in operation of council buildings. The previous closure of Grafton Multi-Storey Car Park (MSCP) has been a clear illustration of this challenge. With the structure being life expired and closed for safety reasons, despite decades of ongoing maintenance at great cost.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-14","committee":"Joint Strategic Committee","paper_title":"Capital Strategy 2026-2029.docx","paper_url":"https://democracy.adur-worthing.gov.uk/documents/s17257/17257.pdf","headline":"Worthing requested Exceptional Financial Support from central government; the capital strategy is now built on that foundation.","detail":"The document states the Financial Strategy 'provided the necessary basis for Worthing's request for Exceptional Financial Support (EFS) from central government and continues to guide the organisation's response to ongoing budgetary pressures.' The capital strategy is a child of that EFS request, meaning Worthing's spending plan is built on admitting it cannot balance its budget without government intervention.","category":"reserves","matters":"EFS is a public signal of financial failure. A council that has sought exceptional support is demonstrating that mainstream financing cannot cover recurrent pressures. The capital strategy flowing from it may optimistic about funding sources or sustainability.","figures":"Worthing general fund capital: \u00a31.5m per year (unchanged since 2024/25, but 'provisional' and 'subject to further review').","quote":"The Financial Strategy adopted in July 2024 remains the primary framework for managing the financial challenges facing both Councils. It provided the necessary basis for Worthing's request for Exceptional Financial Support (EFS) from central government and continues to guide the organisation's response to ongoing budgetary pressures.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-14","committee":"Joint Strategic Committee","paper_title":"Capital Strategy 2026-2029.docx","paper_url":"https://democracy.adur-worthing.gov.uk/documents/s17257/17257.pdf","headline":"Both councils have abandoned their commercial property investment strategy because it failed to generate sustainable income.","detail":"The document states 'Historically, the Councils made strategic investments in Commercial Property with the long-term intention of generating a sustainable income stream. This income was designed to mitigate the significant reductions in Government Funding for service delivery.' Both councils removed all remaining unspent budgets for commercial investment in 2024/25. Commercial debt still represents 32% of Worthing's total debt and 42% of Adur's, but is being run down. This is a recognition that the strategy did not work as planned.","category":"capital","matters":"Councils used commercial property borrowing to plug revenue budget gaps. The fact they have stopped new investment and removed budgets suggests prior investments underperformed or that revenue pressure is now too acute to absorb the capital costs. Either signals deteriorating financial health.","figures":"Worthing: commercial debt 32% of total (2024/25), declining to 26% (2028/29). Adur: 42% of total declining to 32%.","quote":"Historically, the Councils made strategic investments in Commercial Property with the long-term intention of generating a sustainable income stream. This income was designed to mitigate the significant reductions in Government Funding for service delivery. However, Adur and Worthing have decided not to pursue new commercial investment and in the 2024/25 year, as part of the appraisal of both councils capital aspirations the unspent budgets set aside for commercial investment were removed from the capital plans.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-14","committee":"Joint Strategic Committee","paper_title":"Capital Strategy 2026-2029.docx","paper_url":"https://democracy.adur-worthing.gov.uk/documents/s17257/17257.pdf","headline":"Both councils are moving to fund capital spending through rising borrowing rather than capital receipts.","detail":"Worthing's external debt is forecast to rise from \u00a3208.4m (2024/25 actual) to \u00a3245.8m by 2028/29. Adur's rises from \u00a3180.3m to \u00a3224.4m. Neither council can rely on capital receipts \u2014 the document explicitly states 'the proceeds of asset sales are now widely expected to be used to reduce the level of council indebtedness', closing off a major funding source. Both councils are moving to a 'more constrained capital approach'.","category":"borrowing","matters":"Rising debt against shrinking capital income signals financial stress. Lenders need to understand whether borrowing costs can be sustained against the wider revenue budget pressure the paper acknowledges.","figures":"Worthing external debt: \u00a3208.388m (2024/25) to \u00a3245.843m (2028/29). Adur external debt: \u00a3180.299m to \u00a3224.377m. Annual capital budgets: Worthing \u00a31.5m, Adur \u00a31.0m, both 'provisional' and 'subject to further review'.","quote":"Although the Councils have been able to fund some of its capital programme from capital receipts in recent years, the proceeds of asset sales are now widely expected to be used to reduce the level of council indebtedness. Therefore the Councils will need to explore new sources of funding, particularly for major capital schemes.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-07-14"},"name":"Worthing","findings_total":46,"news_drafts_total":3}}